# Architecture & module boundaries

Modular monolith. One FastAPI app, one package per bounded context under `backend/app/modules/`.

## Module layout (every module)
```
modules/<name>/
  router.py        # FastAPI routes: HTTP only, thin, pydantic in/out, authz deps
  service.py       # business logic (the only place it lives)
  repository.py    # DB access for THIS module's tables only
  schemas.py       # pydantic DTOs (request/response)
  models.py        # SQLAlchemy models for THIS module
  tests/
```

## Dependency direction (never invert)
```
router → service → repository → DB
orchestration → tools → domain services   (LLM sees tools, not services)
```
- Routers contain **no business logic**; delegate to services.
- Services never import `fastapi.Request`/`Response` or SQL directly — go through the repository.
- **No cross-module data access:** module A calls module B's *service*, never B's repository/models.
- Domain/business code must not import: any LLM SDK, `httpx` for scraping, or the orchestrator.
- Shared cross-cutting code lives in `backend/app/core/` (config, db session, logging, security, deps).

## Boundaries that must stay independent
- `llm` (provider abstraction) ← used by `orchestration`, never by `books`/`reader`/etc.
- `tools` wrap domain services with schema+authz+audit; the orchestrator only ever calls tools.
- `scraping` owns all outbound-to-source fetching; other modules request scraping via its service/jobs.

When adding a feature, decide which module owns it. If none fits, propose a new module (update phase-0 §11).
